The finding's mechanism is wrong, and its evidence comes from configurations A9 does not use.

(1) What the check measures. In src/itrea/analysis/backoff.py:637-641, end_ref is stop.t (or the budget) and the slack is max_launch_s + 0.25. engine.py:2364-2374 `finish()` does take wall after `lp_worker.stop()` and a final `_gen_record`. In the cited run, though, that clean-up cost about 1 ms:
- in traces/raw/dev/neos-960392__A3@cl1__s1__120s__8d01ac13f25f, the last `gen` event is at trace t=21.741;
- wall_s is 21.7336, and wall_start (engine.py:1038) is about 8 ms after t0;
- stop.t is 21.417 (partner_end).
So the 0.317 s excess is the EA's in-generation delay in honouring the stop. That delay comes from the throttled stop poll (engine.py:256 `STOP_POLL_S = 0.25`, engine.py:1556-1561), the launch in flight and the rest of the generation. The spec's M5 bullet pre-registers exactly that quantity: "a stop is honoured within [one launch + 0.25 s]". If a bo1 run showed 0.32 s there, M5 would be flagging what it was written to catch.

(2) The evidence is from other arms. The 0.317 / 0.253 / 0.254 figures are A3@cl1, A3@tp1 and A3@ad1. The 0.35–0.78 s budget-end overshoots are on ca (A3 base, lps0, lps1) and toguru (A3@lp1). None of these is A9's base. bo0/bo1 build on itrea2-dev-rc-3way: the A9 pilot ran hash 0f011a2094a2, and the rc family is 1d12ad490dd0 (rc1/rc1d) plus rc0. Over all raw traces, those runs (rc0 + rc1 + rc1d + a9p, 64 runs) give:
- 0 of 64 with wall_s − end_ref > 0.25 s;
- a maximum of 0.22 s (glass4 A3@rc1 s0);
- on neos-960392 rc1/rc1d: 0.075–0.119 s;
- in the A9 pilot itself: at most 0.169 s.
All of these sit below the slack's 0.25 s floor, before max_launch_s is even added. The finding's "already exceed that" assumes max_launch_s ≈ 0.05 s, which the finding itself says is unknown.

(3) The clean-up path is not material on this evidence. lp_worker.stop() can take up to 2 s only when the HiGHS worker is still alive (seeding.py:396-405). Across all 353 EA summaries in traces/raw the LP state is 'done', 'none' or 'no_solution', never still running. Even chromaticindex A9@a9p, whose LP state is 'no_solution', overran by only 0.001 s.

(4) Both proposed fixes change the pre-registered M5 rule: judging bo1 relative to bo0, or replacing wall_s with the last event time. The current code is a faithful implementation of the stated bound. Disagreeing with how tightly that bound is calibrated is a comment on the design, not a defect in this diff. The scenario as stated (an ordinary A9 end-of-run time failing M5) has no support in the configuration family stage 1 runs.
